## 2a. Reading (facts only)
- crash (SIGKILL of the SUT): all 11 services stopped on S5 within 8–17 ms and came back healthy in ~0.605 s, ready 1–5 ms later; post-recovery baseline ok on all 22 crash cells.
- live-local crash: 0 pre-fault tasks lost on all 11 (checked = 1, or 7 for task services).
- volatile crash: lost == checked on all 11 (in-memory storage dies with the process). This is BY DESIGN of volatile mode and is NOT a durability pass; `ok` stays True
  only because the FR definition of `ok` (unchanged) treats volatile loss as expected. It is reported, not hidden.
- storage-outage: all 11 stopped on S5 readiness_failure (/ready = 503 "storage timeout") ~1.0 s after the fault — including services whose endpoints never touch storage,
  because readiness checks storage for the whole process. Ready again 0.92–1.03 s after SIGCONT (api-sse 0.005 s); 0 pre-fault tasks lost; 0 failed k6 requests during.
  outcome=stopped_and_recovered everywhere; no cell gave `unaffected_no_stop`.
- CORRECTION of an earlier working note: "readiness does not check storage" is WRONG — it does (503 storage timeout). Log 368 recorded S3 after ~9.9 s for the default
  service; here S5 fires first at ~1 s. The difference between the two runs is NOT investigated in this batch and is recorded as unexplained.
- resources during the fault window (in-process sampler, D-144): fds 20–32, PSS 68.9–75.9 MB, status `measured` in all 33 reports. Windows are ~10–20 s: far too short
  for any leak statement — no leak claim is made from FR.

## 2b. What FR on this box cannot measure (declared)
- network partition / packet loss / latency injection between SUT and storage (single host, loopback only).
- managed-Supabase failure modes (connection pool exhaustion, failover, regional outage) — local PostgREST only.
- multi-worker / multi-instance failover, load-balancer health-check behaviour, rolling restart: one worker, no LB (worker count change needs owner decision, D-142).
- disk-full, OOM-kill under memory pressure, clock skew: not exercised.
- LLM / tool adapter outages against real providers: fake provider only (declared since log 368).
- recovery under production-level load: the fault was injected at the `normal` profile only.

## 3. Harness defect found and fixed: SSE TTFB probe crashed FR for api-sse
root cause: in scenario crash the SSE probe runs after k6 while the SUT is dead; http.client raised ConnectionRefusedError, uncaught, killing the runner (no report).
second defect found while fixing: `ok and all(s == 200 for s in statuses)` — all([]) is True, so an empty probe would have PASSED.
fix (scripts/r177_per_service.py): per-probe errors recorded {probe, error}; status measured|partial|not_measured; `_sse_probe_ok` requires status=measured AND non-empty AND all 200.
